# Redirect map for the Little Eagle site.
#
# This file mirrors the `[[redirects]]` rules in netlify.toml, because a site that
# is deployed WITHOUT a build step (a branch deploy of the prebuilt output, or a
# direct draft deploy) reads _redirects and does not read netlify.toml. Both lists
# are kept identical — tests/redirect-map-test.mjs asserts that.
#
# Design (approved modernization IA):
#   * the site consolidates to 5 routes: / , /register , /physical , /contact ,
#     /thankyou  (+ the branded 404)
#   * About and Fields are no longer standalone destinations — their content lives
#     on homepage sections, so their old URLs redirect to the HOMEPAGE. The homepage
#     navigation still links internally to /#about and /#fields.
#   * every legacy URL keeps working, in one hop
#
# Every rule is FORCED (`301!`). Netlify SHADOWS an unforced rule when a real file
# exists at the same path (see Netlify's redirect "shadowing" docs), and the legacy
# `.html` files do exist on a parity build. Forcing is therefore the only form that
# is correct in every case, and applying it uniformly means a future file appearing
# at one of these paths can never silently de-force a rule.
#
# NEVER PUT A FRAGMENT IN A DESTINATION. A redirect destination must be a token that
# begins with `/` (or an absolute http/https URL). `"/#about"` begins with a quote,
# so Netlify's parser rejects the line — "The destination path/URL must start with
# \"/\", \"http:\" or \"https:\"" — and then DROPS THE RULE SILENTLY, which is a 404
# for a real visitor. Quoting does not help: tokens are whitespace-separated and a
# `#` that begins a token is a comment; there is no quote syntax. A fragment can
# only be reached by the browser AFTER the redirect, never by the rule itself. Hence
# the retired routes below go to `/`, and the homepage nav carries the anchors.
# Enforced by tests/redirect-map-test.mjs and tests/redirect-retired-routes-test.mjs,
# the latter cross-checked against Netlify's own parser when it is installed.

/index.html              /                        301!

# --- Retired routes: About and Fields now live on the homepage ---------------
# Destination is `/` on purpose — see the fragment warning above. One rule also
# covers the trailing-slash form (`/about/`), as Netlify matches it.
/pages/about.html        /                        301!
/pages/about             /                        301!
/about.html              /                        301!
/about                   /                        301!

/pages/fields.html       /                        301!
/pages/fields            /                        301!
/fields.html             /                        301!
/fields                  /                        301!

# --- Form routes: same forms, now at top-level URLs --------------------------
/pages/register.html     /register                301!
/pages/register          /register                301!

/pages/physical.html     /physical                301!
/pages/physical          /physical                301!

/pages/contact.html      /contact                 301!
/pages/contact           /contact                 301!

# The registration form navigates the browser here after a successful submit, so
# this one must never break.
/pages/thankyou.html     /thankyou                301!
/pages/thankyou          /thankyou                301!
